It's written in C, using the paradigms and conventions of 1980 or 
thereabouts.  I would not call it badly written.  On the contrary 
its style is quite good according to the standards in use when it 
was written.  Current standards are not really better, just 
different.  

Current coding standards have to be understood as absolutes that 
prevent bad uses of certain things by forbidding the thing entirely.
As an analogy, it's as though someone noticed that driving screws 
using hammers was bad, and completely banned the use of screws 
to prevent it.  A later generation of carpenters sees stuff that 
was put together with screws and says it's badly built, mostly because
they don't know how to use screwdrivers anymore and assume that screws 
can only be hammered. 

Nethack uses globals in good data-directed style, but data-directed 
style is almost unknown these days.  It completely avoids the uses 
of globals that got global variables banned in most coding standards, 
but modern programmers still see global variables, and being mostly 
ignorant of data-directed style they say the code is badly written.

Briefly; the information encoded as globals is game state information, 
which can reasonably be expected to affect the operation of any 
routine anywhere.  It answers questions about the state of the game
succinctly and directly.  This means most nethack procedures don't 
have inter-program modularity -- take them out of nethack and its 
global environment, and they won't work.  But they are perfectly 
modular in the context of nethack itself, and experience teaches 
that inter-program modularity is rarely as valuable as hoped. 

No global variables are used to pass information other than real 
game state, or to create an "information side channel" between 
procedures, or duplicate information passed in arguments or held 
in other variables.  These things are not good DD style and 
subvert abstraction boundaries, and are the reasons most modern 
standards ban global variables all together. 

Also, it uses pre-ansi function prototypes.  This is because it 
predates ansi.  Of course ansi function prototypes are required 
by virtually all coding style guides today. 

A lot of its functions are longer than you'd currently allow 
functions to get.  This is for two reasons.  First, stack space 
was precious at the time and compilers didn't do auto-inlining 
of small functions.  Second, a lot of functionality (exceptions
and special cases) has been added to the code since those 
functions were first written.  The architecture of the nethack 
codebase didn't (and doesn't) have callback extension hooks that 
allow you to extend functionality by adding functions without 
modifying existing code; the technique, now firmly entrenched in 
the modern Object-Oriented paradigm, was considered risky and 
baroque at the time (and is distinctly ugly and hard in C).  As 
a result, nearly all of the features that have been added since 
the game was "complete" in its first version or two have been 
added by adding code inline, in the middle of functions. 

I looked at the nethack codebase about a year ago, and found it 
surprisingly good although somewhat overwhelmed by exceptions 
and special cases.  

Anyway; archaic I'll grant, written to standards other than the 
current set, and extended with a lot of functionality without any 
refactoring to accommodate the new functionality in structured 
ways.  But that's not the same as being badly written.  That's just
a case of being a "mature" program. 

